A||||9||N_Man||Keeping a unique car from being written off||||9||
Z||000000||N_Man||01-19-2001||03:29 PM||||Well here is the situation, my dad was in an accident (not his fault, a cop came and verified that) but his car an Alfa Romeo Milano got wrecked, its probably gonna be a write off.  damage isnt super bad but the blue book value isnt high either.<P>The car is fairly unique and a replacement would be next to impossible to find.  So given that its a unique car does anybody have suggestions on what could be done to keep the car from being written off and keep it on the road.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Fformula88||01-19-2001||04:14 PM||||Even if its "totalled out" by the insurance co., it doesn't mean you have to give it up.  That simply means they will only pay uou up to its current value, and not the full repair costs.  The downside is you will have to deduct the "salvage value" from the check they cut to you.  But that way you can keep the car and get it fixed.  Unfortunately, some of the repair costs will be coming out of your pocket.  Since its a rare car, I am sure this wouldn't be too much of a problem.  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>John<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">    <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1997 Ford Probe SE<BR>AOL IM: Fformula88||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||01-19-2001||04:37 PM||||sort of a long story...a girl I know has a Volvo 4 dr sedan, she was in a fender bender and damaged the hood, fender, front bumper, headlites, signal lites, grill etc. The blue book on the car was like $3500. she owned it since new and had very low milage. The parts are very expensive for them so the total repair bill for another shop was over $4,000. They paid off the balance on a loan for it like $2,000, gave her $1500 check. She bought back the car for $200. I found the parts all used for less than $700. Then she had the car inspected and relicenced. Ok with the math she got her car paid off, got it back almost the way it was before it was wrecked and still had money left over to spend. so all in all she made like $2500 to have the accident, and still has her same car looking like new.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||01-19-2001||05:31 PM||||Pretty much what they said. Try to keep the title from changing hands so it doesn't show totalled or salvage. <P>Negotiate the value for the car. Mention work done in the last year or so, exceptional condition, etc... <P>Negotiate the salvage value they want to deduct to let you keep it if it is very much. On older cars you are doing them a favor by taking it. They will get very little from a salvage yard and may even have to pay someone to take it.<P>I don't know about Canada, but in the US the other guy's insurance is usually anxious to get you to sign off on it when you're not at fault. They don't want you calling 1-800-LAW-SUIT. Gives you a little more leverage in negotiations...||||reg||1
Z||000004||baldlobo||01-19-2001||06:20 PM||||Do you have Autopac in Alberta?||||reg||5
Z||000005||N_Man||01-19-2001||07:29 PM||||first of all thanks for the suggestions, and keep em coming.<P>baldlobo:<BR>I have no clue what autopac is...<P>How about getting an appraiser to estimate the true value of the car.  Would an insurance company recognise this if it is higher then the blue book?<P>Basicly the car will be fixed, were just trying to keep it in the other insurance companys wallet, compleatly.||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||01-20-2001||09:56 AM||||One suggestion to keep it from having a "salvage" title is to first find out what they will pay you, then let them know that you will accept that amount if they value the car high enough to not to total it out.  I have never done this, but I have a few cars that I would try this with if anything like your situation happens.  (61 Studebaker Lark, 67 Buick skylark conv, 86 Fiero GT)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||01-20-2001||10:59 PM||||If its truly that collectable having an appraised value may help, but they won't give you that much straight out.  If its a lot of money, it may be worth having an attorney handle the case.  Insurance companies are sued all the time over the value of cars.<P>My dad is going through a similar situation with his 87 coupe (that I wrecked).  The car is actually still driveable, but the repair estimate is $3000 and the car isn't valued that high in the blue book.  The ins. company (the other driver was at fault, and its his co.) isn't going to pay the total for the repair.  My father is an attorney, and thinks he will have to sue them just to get the fair market value for the car.<P>Like Roger's story, however, it looks like we will make out well.  The car is damaged in the front end... It has 130K miles on it, and the exterior is in bad shape.  There are scratches from a guard rail rub on the passenger side from a previous accident, the rear bumper is damaged, someone backed over the drivers side fender with a big SUV plus numerous other scratches.  Today we found a junked 87 coupe with its body intact and in flawless shape.  So we are going to replace most of the body panels on his car, and get the car completely repainted.  IF the ins. company gives us the blue book (about $2000) for the car, we should be able to get it fully repaired and completely painted for under that!  In the end the car will be much better than it was prior to the accident!!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||baldlobo||01-21-2001||02:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by N_Man:<BR><B>first of all thanks for the suggestions, and keep em coming.<P>baldlobo:<BR>I have no clue what autopac is...<P>How about getting an appraiser to estimate the true value of the car.  Would an insurance company recognise this if it is higher then the blue book?<P>Basicly the car will be fixed, were just trying to keep it in the other insurance companys wallet, compleatly.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That answers my question.<P>PS: That's insurence in manitoba.||||reg||1
